Conquering Decision Fatigue: Simplify Your Choices for a Better Day

A few years ago, it seemed interesting why some people always wear the same clothes and have the same morning routine. Then, I came across the term Decision Fatigue.

According to psychiatrist Dr. MacLean, decision fatigue is a decline in our ability to make decisions as a result of making too many decisions in our daily lives.

If we look at the numbers, a person makes more than 35,000 decisions in a day, and it takes a lot of energy and time.

As soon as we get up in the morning, we start making decisions. For example, what to eat for breakfast, what to wear today, which bag to use, how to style our hair, how to go to work or study, etc.

Ultimately, by the time we start the main part of our day, we have decision fatigue and it becomes difficult to make more important decisions.

One of the solutions is to automate our daily life as much as possible through habits. For example, I try automate the habits of what to do straight after waking up and before I going to sleep. I have also scheduled which part of the day to do university classes (majors) and additional classes (minors). The color of the clothes I buy is also limited to similar colors.

If you find yourself starting your day with countless choices, try this: instead of asking, “Should I drink tea or lemon tea?” start your day with, “Today is my day to drink tea!”

